# Time and Work: Rates, Efficiency, and Team Effort

> If A builds a wall in 12 days and B in 18, how long together?
> Three sentences of setup and one line of algebra answer it. Time and
> work problems convert every worker into a *rate*, add the rates, and
> divide the job by the total. From there the chapter branches into
> pipes, alternate days, wages, and the ever-popular "man-days"
> equations — all one idea wearing different hats.

## 1. From Times to Rates

[definition]
If a worker completes a job in $n$ days, the **one-day work** (rate)
is $\frac{1}{n}$ of the job per day. Workers' rates simply add:
together they do $\frac{1}{a} + \frac{1}{b} + \cdots$ of the job each
day.
[/definition]

@math label="eq:rate-add"
\frac{1}{T} = \frac{1}{a} + \frac{1}{b} \quad\Rightarrow\quad T = \frac{ab}{a+b}
@/math

**Worked example.** A: 12 days, B: 18 days, together:

@math label="eq:together"
\frac{1}{12} + \frac{1}{18} = \frac{3+2}{36} = \frac{5}{36} \Rightarrow T = \frac{36}{5} = 7.2\ \text{days}
@/math

The harmonic-style result $\frac{ab}{a+b}$ gives
$\frac{12 \times 18}{30} = 7.2$ immediately.

## 2. The LCM Method: Work in Units

Instead of fractions, set the **total work = LCM of the days**.
For 12 and 18, take 36 units: A does 3 units/day, B does 2 units/day,
together 5 — $36/5 = 7.2$ days. The method shines when several
workers join and leave:

**Worked example.** A and B begin a 60-unit job (A: 4/day, B:
6/day — from 15 and 10 days). After 3 days (30 units done), C joins
(C: 5/day). Remaining 30 units at 15/day needs 2 more days — total 5
days. No fraction ever appears.

## 3. Efficiency and the Chain of Workers

Efficiency is the inverse of time — twice the efficiency means half
the days:

@math label="eq:efficiency"
\text{time} \times \text{efficiency} = \text{constant}
@/math

**Worked example.** A is twice as efficient as B and finishes 8 days
earlier. Let B take $t$ days, so A takes $\frac{t}{2}$: then
$t - \frac{t}{2} = 8$, giving $t = 16$ and A takes 8 days.

**Men-women-children chains.** "3 men = 5 women" style equivalences
convert everyone into one currency (say, units/day of the *same*
job) before adding.

## 4. The MDH Formula for Groups

[important]
Work is proportional to men $\times$ days $\times$ hours $\times$
per-hour efficiency. For two crews on the same kind of job:

@math label="eq:mdh"
\frac{M_1 D_1 H_1}{W_1} = \frac{M_2 D_2 H_2}{W_2}
@/math

Add efficiency ratios $\frac{E_1}{E_2}$ as an extra factor when
crews differ in skill.
[/important]

**Worked example.** 12 men working 8 hours/day finish a wall in 15
days. How long will 18 men working 10 hours/day take (same wall)?

@math label="eq:mdh-example"
D_2 = \frac{12 \times 8 \times 15}{18 \times 10} = 8\ \text{days}
@/math

## 5. Pipes and Cisterns

Filling pipes are positive rates, draining pipes negative:

@math label="eq:pipes"
\frac{1}{T} = \frac{1}{f_1} + \frac{1}{f_2} - \frac{1}{d}
@/math

**Worked example.** Pipe A fills in 6 h, B fills in 8 h, C empties in
12 h; all open:

@math label="eq:pipes-example"
\frac{1}{6} + \frac{1}{8} - \frac{1}{12} = \frac{4+3-2}{24} = \frac{5}{24}
\Rightarrow T = \frac{24}{5} = 4.8\ \text{h}
@/math

Tank-capacity LCM (24 units) makes it visual: A gives 4/h, B 3/h, C
takes 2/h — net 5/h.

## 6. Alternating Days and Wages

**Alternate days.** A (3 units/day) and B (2 units/day) alternate on a
12-unit job starting with A. Two days yield 5 units; after 4 such
cycles (8 days) 20 units — overshoot means the 12-unit job finishes
inside: after 2 days 5, 4 days 10, then A's day adds 3 $\Rightarrow$
done on day 5. Always simulate day-pairs, then handle the leftover.

**Wages.** Pay divides in the ratio of work done, i.e. the ratio of
efficiencies. If A and B finish a job in 6 days with rates 3:2, wages
split $3:2$ regardless of who worked which day — unless the question
specifies unequal attendance.

[example]
A alone finishes in 10 days; A and B together in 6. B's rate:
$\frac{1}{6} - \frac{1}{10} = \frac{5-3}{30} = \frac{1}{15}$, so B
alone needs 15 days. Every "find the missing worker" question is this
subtraction of rates — the single most used line in the chapter.
[/example]

[summary]
Convert every time into a rate: one-day work $= \frac{1}{n}$, add
rates for collaboration ($T = \frac{ab}{a+b}$ for two workers), and
prefer the LCM-units method for multi-stage problems. Efficiency is
inverse to time; groups scale by
$\frac{M_1 D_1 H_1}{W_1} = \frac{M_2 D_2 H_2}{W_2}$. Pipes and
cisterns are the same engine with signed rates. Alternating-day
puzzles are solved by simulating day-pairs and cleaning the leftover,
and wages split by efficiency ratio. Find the rate; everything else is
arithmetic.
[/summary]
